// ===================== 23.4 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('23.4  Three sentences: what, who says so, what is not known'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Stop each student at **thirty seconds**, without apology; the stage does not work if the clock is soft. Most of the room will not have reached what happened, because most of the room is reading the page back in its printed order. Then have the class diagnose each attempt with one of three words — _what_, _who_, _not known_ — and the diagnosis is almost always the third.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** who says so   **2** what   **3** what is not known   **4** who says so   **5** what   **6** what is not known. ⚠ Note why **4** is a _who_ and not a _what_: _the committee minuted it on the twenty-seventh_ is a sentence about the evidence, not about the event — and it is the strongest kind of sentence 2, because the source is a document with a date.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **A** – four minutes; and the sentence nobody writes is **what is not known**.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** Because sentence one is information and sentence two is its credit: **sentence three is the only one of the three that tells anybody what to do next**.   **2** His manager said one word — _right_ — and then asked him to go and find out the third sentence, which is the first piece of work anybody has given him on the strength of something he said.'));
out.push(NOTE('The mechanism in this cycle is worth stating to the class as a mechanism, because it is the most directly useful sentence in the unit: **the person who writes the third sentence is the person who gets asked to go and find out.** Everybody manages sentence one, most people manage two, and almost nobody writes three — so writing three is cheap and conspicuous. The second thing to teach is the diagnosis of the four minutes, which Omar gives himself and which students will not give themselves: the four minutes were not too long, **they were in the bulletin\'s order instead of ours**, and he had not decided what the item was about before he started talking. That is the same difficulty as 22.1 and it has the same remedy — twenty minutes with a pen before anybody asks.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _Four samples were released eighteen hours late on 29 October. The laboratory\'s own bulletin says so, and the technician concerned wrote the item. What is not known is how often bags have been left on that shelf, because no record is kept._',
  '**2** _The second bus on the Harasta service is under review. The transport office told the depot on 28 November, and the service is theirs to review. What is not known is whether a decision will be taken before the January timetable is printed._',
  '**3** _My mother is moving to my sister\'s house in February. She said so herself on Friday and the arrangement is hers. What is not known is whether the Suwayda house will be let or kept empty — nobody has said._',
  '**4** _Answers on the forum will carry names from January. The editors have confirmed it, and the page is theirs. What is not known is whose name goes on an answer that two people wrote._',
  '**5** _I shall take the B2 course in the spring. I have decided it and nobody else is involved. What is not known is whether the Tuesday class will run, and the centre has not said._',
]));
out.push(A('Check two things only. **Sentence 2 must have both halves** — the source **and** why it would know — because the second half is the one everybody drops, and a source with no reason to know is just somebody who also read something. And **sentence 3 must hold the gap open rather than guess at it**: ⚠ _what is not known is whether…_ and not _I think it will probably…_. Then make the class notice item 3 and item 5: the three sentences work on a family and on oneself, which is what proves they are not a journalist\'s trick.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**B:** The maintenance contract for the eleven sites will be put out to tender in the spring.',
  '**B:** The directorate\'s own December bulletin says so, and the contract is theirs to tender.',
  '**B:** What is not known is whether the present arrangement continues until the tender is decided.',
  '**A:** The weakest was sentence two, because you said "the bulletin says so" and stopped — the second half is what makes it the only possible source. Again — twenty-five seconds.',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('Six or seven of the eight third sentences contain **whether**, and the reason is worth saying: almost every gap that matters in a piece of news is a **yes-or-no question somebody has not answered**, and _whether_ is the one word that holds it open without guessing at it. The alternative openings — _nobody has said who…_, _no date has been given for…_ — cover the rest. Collect any third sentence that contains _probably_ or _I think_ and strike it out: that is a fourth sentence in the third sentence\'s place.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _What: the storage-temperature requirement for reagent lots is being rewritten. Who says so: the senior technician, on 2 December, and she is on the group doing it. What is not known: whether the new wording will apply to lots already on the shelf — she does not know either. To fill it I should have to ask the group\'s secretary one question, and I could do it this week._',
  '**b** _My partner\'s second sentence was "the bulletin says so" and nothing else. The missing half: and the contract is the directorate\'s to tender, so they are the only people who could say it. Without that half the sentence is just a note of where he read it._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Omar\'s manager put a bulletin on his desk in November and said: what does this say? And he talked for four minutes. Nour asked what was in the four minutes, and it was everything on the page in the order it was printed, including two things that had nothing to do with them — and at the end the manager said: so is the contract changing or not? Which was the question, and Omar had not answered it, because he had not decided what the item was about before he opened his mouth. Ms Huda gave him three sentences and only three: what happened or what is claimed; who says so and how they would know; and what is not known. Jad asked for his, and Omar gave it: the maintenance contract for the eleven sites will be put out to tender in the spring; the directorate\'s own December bulletin says so, and the contract is theirs to tender; and what is not known is whether the present arrangement continues until the tender is decided — the bulletin does not say, and that is the only part that affects any of them. Lubna said twenty-five seconds and Omar said twenty-three. And when he gave it to his manager, the manager said one word, "right", and then asked him to go and find out the third sentence, which is the first piece of work anybody has given him on the strength of something he said. Ms Huda said that is why the third sentence matters: sentence one is information and sentence two is its credit, and sentence three is the only one of the three that tells anybody what to do next — everybody writes one, most people manage two, and almost nobody writes three, and the person who writes three is the person who gets asked to go and find out. Omar said the hard part is not the length but deciding what the item is about before you speak, and Ms Huda said that is the same difficulty as 22.1, with the same remedy: twenty minutes with a pen before anybody asks you.']));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('✗ What is not known is whether to continue the arrangement. → ✓ _whether the arrangement **continues**_ _(a clause, not an infinitive)_.   ✗ It is not known that who will pay. → ✓ _it is not known **who** will pay_.   ✗ The bulletin says so, and it is their contract to tender it. → ✓ _the contract is **theirs to tender**_.   ✗ I think probably the arrangement continues. → ✓ _what is not known is **whether** it continues_ _(no guess in sentence 3)_.   ✗ Nobody has said when will the tender be. → ✓ _when the tender **will be**_ _(indirect, 19.3)_.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** That the contract is **theirs to tender**, so they are the only people who could say it. That is the second half of sentence 2, and it is the half that turns a note of where he read something into a source.'));
out.push(A('**2** She said — and he marks it as her word and not his — that the present arrangement _"carries on as it is"_ until a tender is awarded. What she **could not do** is put it in writing, because no date has been set for the tender itself. So one half of the third sentence is answered by a person he can name and the other half is not answered by anybody.'));
out.push(A('**3** They were **in the bulletin\'s order instead of ours**, and he had not decided what the item was about before he started talking. He does not say they were too long.'));
out.push(A('Two things to lift out of the model. First, the quotation marked as the speaker\'s word — _this is her word, not mine_ — which is 23.1 and 23.2 together: the source is named and the verb is _says_. Second, the request: **eight words, in writing, from a named office** (19.4), and what it buys — eleven sites able to plan for the spring. A student who writes _could the directorate please clarify the position_ has asked for nothing; Omar has asked for one sentence somebody can type.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
