out.push(S('Stage 7 · Exam practice – B1 Preliminary Writing Part 1 (sample, 176 words)'));
out.push(A('Out of 20, **140 words or more**, and all **four** notes must be covered: the first thing to look at and why; what is wrong with _reportedly_; what to do with something three people have said; and **one example from your own reading**. The fourth note is the one scripts drop — a script that gives the three pieces of advice and no instance has not covered it, however well written.'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  'Dear Sam,',
  'Your brother and you are arguing about the news when the argument is about **sentences**, so start there.',
  'The first thing to look at is whether there is **anybody in the sentence**. _According to the works foreman, Nabil Zahra, seventeen of the nineteen presses are out of use_ has a man and a figure in it. Three things can now go wrong for whoever wrote that — the foreman can deny it, the figure can be counted again, and the name can be telephoned — so the sentence cost somebody something. That is what you are believing: a cost that has been accepted.',
  'Which is why _reportedly_ is the problem. Reported by whom? There is nobody in the sentence, so there is nobody to ask, and it cannot be contradicted — which is also why it cannot be relied on. It sounds official and it is the cheapest word on the page.',
  'And when your brother says three people told him, ask each of them one question: **who told you?** Almost always the three turn into one, because they are repeating each other. Three mouths is one rumour, not three pieces of evidence.',
  'My example: a bulletin here printed _many depots are reportedly receiving double allocations_ — no figure, no source — on the **same page** as the sentence about the presses. Same paper, same day. The sentence is the unit, not the newspaper.',
  'Lubna',
]));
out.push(NOTE('Writing Part 1 is an email with four notes and the mark is lost on **coverage**: number the notes in the margin, tick each as it is answered, and give each one its own paragraph. ⚠ Note that the fourth note here asks for something from the candidate\'s **own reading**, which is the kind of note that tempts a candidate to invent — and an invented example is usually detectable because it has no source and no date. The honest cheap version is always available: name the publication, the month and the sentence. And the last line of this sample is doing the fourth note twice over, because it draws the conclusion the example is for: _the sentence is the unit, not the newspaper._'));
out.push(S('Unit 23 check  ·  A  Grammar and vocabulary'));
out.push(A('**1** a   **2** b   **3** c   **4** a   **5** b   **6** c   **7** a   **8** b'));
out.push(A('_Notes:_ **1** _admit_ + _-ing_. **2** _deny_ + _-ing_. **3** _refuse_ + infinitive — ⚠ the three forms in 1–3 are the examinable core of 23.2. **4** _According to_ + noun. **5** _is said **to have been** re-tested_ — ⚠ _to be_ would make it now and _that it was_ needs _it is said_. **6** _told me_ — ⚠ _announced me_ and _said me_ are both wrong, and both are heard (18.5). **7** _no confirmation **that**_ before a clause. **8** _it is **expected** that_ — a past participle, not a present participle.'));
out.push(S('Unit 23 check  ·  B  Listening (Part 3, note completion) – Track 23.15'));
out.push(A('**1** **nine**   **2** **three**   **3** **claim** _(or_ claimed_)_   **4** **confirmed** _(it)_   **5** **passengers**   **6** **opinion**'));
out.push(A('Gaps **2** and **4** are the pair to replay. In **2** three numbers are spoken close together — nine sentences, two facts, three with nobody in them — and a candidate who has not read the question takes the first number they hear. In **4** the required word is a **verb**, and the script says _confirmed_ only once, inside a long sentence about the item refuting its own verb; candidates who are still writing gap 3 lose it. ⚠ Gap **3** accepts _claim_ or _claimed_: the script says both the quoted sentence and the bare word.'));
out.push(NOTE('Listening Part 3 is note completion with six gaps, and the marks go on **spelling and on word class**, not on comprehension: a candidate who writes _confirm_ for gap 4 or _opinions_ for gap 6 has understood the talk and lost the mark. Two habits. Read the whole page of notes in the pause and mark what **kind** of word each gap needs — a number, a verb, a plural noun — because the kind is visible from the sentence around the gap. And write the word you hear, not a synonym: Part 3 is almost always the speaker\'s own word, and a candidate who improves it is wrong.'));
